from pathlib import Path
import json,math,statistics
b=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;d=json.loads((b/'summary.json').read_text());assert len(d['runs'])==4
agg=d['aggregate'];assert all(agg[p]['runs']==2 for p in ['rr','qos'])
def flow(p,f):return agg[p]['flows'][f]
def loss(p,f):
 q=flow(p,f);return 100*(q['sent']-q['received'])/q['sent']
def reduction(f):return 100*(1-flow('qos',f)['mean_ms']/flow('rr',f)['mean_ms'])
values={p:[flow(p,f)['mean_ms'] for f in ['dl_control','dl_video','dl_bulk']] for p in ['rr','qos']}
mx=max(v for vv in values.values() for v in vv);step=100 if mx>200 else 50 if mx>100 else 20 if mx>40 else 10 if mx>20 else 5
all_received=all(q['sent']==q['received'] for p in agg.values() for q in p['flows'].values())
assert all_received,'Keep loss visible and revise caption before proceeding if any packet is missing.'
q=json.loads((b/'queue-log-examples.json').read_text());assert all(x['logged_full_rlc_queue_events']==0 for x in q),'Persistent overflow must be explained before accepting operating point.'
notes='''RTX 5090 experiment: brief video congestion, measured 14 Oct 2026 SGT. Source docs/reports/experiments/scheduler-bursty-load-20261014/README.md and summary.json. Four sequential runs RR, QoS, QoS, RR, each with 120 measurement seconds and six seconds of drain. One OCUDU gNB and two srsUEs. Both schedulers retain their original defaults.\n\nDownload UE: 5QI 9, 30 Mb/s constant DL plus 2 Mb/s UL. Interactive UE: 5QI 7, 40 Mb/s video during exponentially distributed ON periods averaging 0.2 s; OFF periods average 0.8 s. Control sends 200-byte packets every 10 ms in both directions. Mean ON/OFF durations are not fixed periodic slots. The expected downlink demand is 38.16 Mb/s including control; the peak is 70.16 Mb/s. Measured cell service capacity in the preceding stress test was about 57 Mb/s, so brief bursts compete for resources but average offered load can drain. Exact realized packet counts are recorded.\n\nRelative to the preceding 70/40 Mb/s sustained overload, only download rate (70 to 30) and mean video ON/OFF duration (2/1 s to 0.2/0.8 s) change. No queue limit, drop policy or scheduler parameter was adjusted. Both policies use the same traffic seed, resources and channel scenario. All four runs are retained, not selected by effect size.\n\nBars show the arithmetic mean of each valid second’s p99 delivered-packet one-way UDP delay, averaged equally over two runs per policy. Empty video seconds are excluded. This is not pooled packet p99, maximum latency or scheduler execution time. All configured DL and UL packets arrive after drain. There are no full-RLC-queue log messages in any run.\n\nRTX 5090, Core Ultra 9 285K, 20 MHz, 15 kHz SCS, 23.04 MS/s, CUDA channel, 23040-sample maximum batches, seed-1 moving robot-ring Sionna scenario at 10 Hz with configured 40 dB AWGN SNR. Affinity: gNB cores 0–7, both UEs share 8–19, broker 20, auxiliary work 22–23. Broker pacing remains enabled and redundant srsUE receive sleep disabled. IQ progress uses cumulative acquired samples over monotonic wall time.\n\nQoS is the default OCUDU proportional-fair scheduler with priority, packet-delay-budget and GBR terms enabled, fairness exponent 2. The non-GBR formula on the slide is approximate; zero-wait and new-UE cases are omitted. Control/video share one default bearer on the interactive UE, so this is prioritization between UEs, not separate scheduling classes for video and control. Their 5QI 7 has 100 ms packet delay budget; download 5QI 9 has 300 ms. These are inputs, not guarantees. Source use_cases/scheduler_benchmark/definitions.py and the native OCUDU scheduler_time_qos.cpp.\n\nTwo repeats at one seed do not establish statistical significance or a general policy ranking. Small ray-solver numeric differences and uncontrolled host background activity remain. Starvation events remain recorded, so no strict zero-miss real-time qualification is claimed. The earlier overloaded case is retained in scheduler-priority-load-20261014.\n'''
notes+='\nWorst observed per-second p99 delay (not maximum packet delay): '+json.dumps({p:{f:max(x for r in d['runs'] if r['policy']==p for x in r['flows'][f]['mean_per_second_p99_series_ms'] if x is not None) for f in ['dl_control','dl_video','dl_bulk']} for p in ['rr','qos']})
notes+='\nDelay and delivery aggregates: '+json.dumps(agg)
notes+='\nTiming evidence: '+json.dumps([{'run':r['run'],'IQ_fraction_nominal':r['observed_iq']['gnb0_p0']['fraction_nominal'],'slots_per_s':r['reported_slots_per_s'],'broker_counters':r['broker_counters']} for r in d['runs']])
a,z=reduction('dl_control'),reduction('dl_video')
finding=f'QoS reduced control delay by {a:.0f}% and video delay by {z:.0f}%.' if min(a,z)>0 else 'Both schedulers carried the brief bursts without packet loss.'
plot={'values':values,'axis_max':math.ceil(mx*1.2/step)*step,'axis_step':step,'burst_caption':'Video bursts: mean 0.2 s ON / 0.8 s OFF','delivery_caption':'Both schedulers delivered every packet','finding':finding,'setup':'RTX 5090 · 20 MHz · 23.04 MS/s · 2 × 120 s/policy · 14 Oct 2026','speaker_notes':notes}
(b/'plot-data.json').write_text(json.dumps(plot,indent=2))
report='# Scheduler comparison with brief video bursts — 14 October 2026\n\nThe previous test saturated both UE queues and produced roughly one-second interactive delays. This experiment instead creates brief congestion that can clear between bursts. The design was saved before execution: 30 Mb/s download, 40 Mb/s video while ON, mean ON/OFF durations 0.2/0.8 seconds. Expected average DL demand including control is 38.16 Mb/s, below the approximately 57 Mb/s cell capacity measured in the previous stress test. Peak demand is 70.16 Mb/s.\n\nNo scheduler settings, queue limits or drop policies change. All four RR/QoS/QoS/RR runs are retained.\n\n## Results\n\n| Metric | Round-robin | QoS-aware |\n|---|---:|---:|\n'
for f,label in [('dl_control','Control delay, ms'),('dl_video','Video delay, ms'),('dl_bulk','Download delay, ms')]:report+=f"| {label} | {flow('rr',f)['mean_ms']:.3f} | {flow('qos',f)['mean_ms']:.3f} |\n"
for f,label in [('dl_control','Control packets received/sent'),('dl_video','Video packets received/sent'),('dl_bulk','Download packets received/sent')]:report+=f"| {label} | {flow('rr',f)['received']}/{flow('rr',f)['sent']} | {flow('qos',f)['received']}/{flow('qos',f)['sent']} |\n"
report+=f'\nControl delay changed by {a:.2f}% and video delay by {z:.2f}% in favor of QoS. All packets, including both uplink streams, arrived after the six-second drain. No full-RLC-queue events appear in any run.\n\nDelay is the mean of per-second p99 delivered-packet one-way UDP delay, equally averaged over two runs. Empty video seconds are excluded. This is not pooled packet p99 or maximum latency.\n\n| UTC run | Policy | Control ms | Video ms | IQ progress / nominal | Channel starvations |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for r in d['runs']:report+=f"| {r['run']} | {r['policy'].upper()} | {r['flows']['dl_control']['mean_per_second_p99_ms']:.3f} | {r['flows']['dl_video']['mean_per_second_p99_ms']:.3f} | {r['observed_iq']['gnb0_p0']['fraction_nominal']*100:.4f}% | {r['broker_counters']['rx_starvations']} |\n"
report+='\n| Largest per-second p99 | Round-robin | QoS-aware |\n|---|---:|---:|\n'
for f,label in [('dl_control','Control, ms'),('dl_video','Video, ms'),('dl_bulk','Download, ms')]:
 peaks={p:max(x for r in d['runs'] if r['policy']==p for x in r['flows'][f]['mean_per_second_p99_series_ms'] if x is not None) for p in ['rr','qos']}
 report+=f"| {label} | {peaks['rr']:.3f} | {peaks['qos']:.3f} |\n"
report+='\nThe worst per-second p99 was not improved by QoS; the reported 24% benefit applies to the mean of the per-second values. Download delay is included to expose the service tradeoff. The scheduler can reduce waiting for the interactive UE by making the download UE wait longer, even when all packets arrive.\n\n## Setup and checks\n\n- RTX 5090 and Core Ultra 9 285K. One gNB and two UEs at a time, two separate 120-second measurements per policy, six seconds drain. Unchanged binaries and scheduler defaults from the [previous experiment](../scheduler-priority-load-20261014/README.md).\n- gNB cores 0–7, UEs share 8–19, broker 20, auxiliary processes 22–23. Existing unrelated containers were preserved.\n- 20 MHz, 15 kHz SCS, 23.04 MS/s, CUDA channel, maximum 23040-sample batches. Seed-1 moving robot-ring Sionna scenario at 10 Hz, configured 40 dB AWGN SNR. Broker pacing stays enabled, redundant UE receive sleep disabled.\n- Download UE 5QI 9: 30 Mb/s DL and 2 Mb/s UL, 1200-byte packets. Interactive UE 5QI 7: 40 Mb/s video, exponentially distributed mean ON/OFF periods 0.2/0.8 seconds, plus 200-byte control packets every 10 ms in both directions.\n- Launcher overrides: `OCUDU_NATIVE_SB_SINGLE_CELL=1`, `OCUDU_NATIVE_SB_UE_RX_SLEEP=false`, `OCUDU_NATIVE_SB_TRAFFIC_OVERRIDES=ue0.dl_bulk.rate_mbps=30,ue1.dl_video.rate_mbps=40,ue1.dl_video.on_mean_s=0.2,ue1.dl_video.off_mean_s=0.8`.\n\nThe configuration comparison verifies identical traffic and CPU allocations across policies, with only scheduler policy differing in gNB configuration. All 120 measurement seconds and 1200 channel grid positions are validated per run. Channel positions, tap counts and delays match; small ray-solver gain/phase differences are retained in `matched_checks`. Observed active processes and CPU affinities confirm sequential single-cell execution.\n\nIQ progress is cumulative acquired samples over monotonic elapsed time, using interior heartbeats. All channel queue-overflow, sample-gap and ZMQ-error counters are zero. Input-starvation events remain; this does not establish strict zero-miss real-time qualification. Two repeats at one seed do not establish statistical significance or a general scheduler ranking.\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Predeclared design](design.json), [summary](summary.json), [resource checks](resource-check.json), [queue checks](queue-log-examples.json), [sequence](sequence-status.log), [analysis](collect.py), [source hashes](source-hashes.json), [raw hashes](raw-hashes.json). Each run folder contains the benchmark report, run settings, gate verdict, policy check and configuration hashes. Earlier overload measurements remain unchanged in their original report.\n'
(b/'README.md').write_text(report)
print(json.dumps(plot|{'speaker_notes':'[saved]'},indent=2))
